    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The most common cause of hearing loss and balance disorders is loss of inner ear sensory hair cells. While sensory organs from non-mammalian species robustly regenerate, previous studies have found no regeneration in the mature mammalian cochlea and only limited regeneration in the mammalian vestibular system. It has recently been found that a subset of non-sensory supporting cells, marked by the protein Lgr5 (leucine rich repeat containing G protein-coupled receptor 5), is capable of spontaneously regenerating lost hair cells in the neonatal mouse cochlea and utricle. However, knowledge of the time-course of cell fate change, differentiation, and proliferation by these hair cell progenitors is lacking. Here we probed the genetic signature of hair cell progenitors to understand the molecular mechanisms that guide hair cell regeneration after damage. A mouse model that allowed for selective ablation of hair cells was used, and immunohistochemical staining of early hair cell markers (Gfi1, Atoh1) and a proliferation marker (Ki67) allowed us to map the transition of supporting cells into hair cells in the neonatal mouse cochlea and utricle after damage. The findings demonstrated that the neonatal utricle and cochlea have an upregulation of early hair cell markers before proliferative markers. These data suggested that regenerating cells in the neonatal mouse inner ear sensory organs follow a similar temporal pattern of regeneration to the inner ear organs from non-mammalian species.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
